Make the power lifecycle transactional and stop discarding its errors

rknpu_power_on() enabled the regulators, the clock bulk and each power
domain in turn and returned the first failure without undoing any of it,
so a failure part way through left the earlier ones enabled with nothing
recording that. It unwinds in reverse now, and only runs state_init()
once the device is actually resumed rather than on the way out of a
failed pm_runtime_get_sync().

rknpu_power_get() left its reference incremented when the power-on it had
just triggered failed, so the count said the hardware was up when it was
not and no later put could correct it. It rolls the count back.

Both ioctl paths then acted on that ignored failure, touching registers
behind clocks that were never enabled. They return the error instead.

Probe powered the hardware up behind the refcount's back, with a direct
rknpu_power_on() against a still-zero count, which is why the earlier
"do not zero it on failure" change did nothing: it was already zero.
Probe takes a counted reference and releases it through rknpu_power_put()
like every other user, so the count describes the hardware throughout and
a power-off that cannot run is retried rather than forgotten.

Probe also discarded what rknpu_devfreq_init() returned. -EOPNOTSUPP just
means there is no OPP table, which is supported, but everything else was
being swallowed - including the -EPROBE_DEFER raised when the OTP holding
the variant selection has not bound yet, which would leave an RK3576S
running the full 900 MHz table.

Remove destroyed the retry workqueue before attempting the power-off, so
a power-off that answered -EBUSY queued its retry onto freed memory, and
it detached the power domains and disabled runtime PM whether or not the
hardware had actually gone down. The workqueue now outlives the attempt,
and a failed power-off leaves the suppliers attached rather than pulling
them out from under live hardware.

Finally, delete rknpu_devfreq_lock() and rknpu_devfreq_unlock(). They
have been empty since the devfreq rewrite - the vendor state they guarded
does not exist here - and kept suggesting the power paths took a lock
they did not.

The failure paths needed the same care as the success path.

Remove cannot refuse: the callback the kernel calls returns void, so an
early return only skipped the rest of the teardown while unbind carried
on regardless - leaving the retry worker alive holding rknpu_dev and
module text. It now quiesces that worker first, powers down second, and
frees third, in that order, and says so loudly if the power-off failed
rather than pretending it was declined. Cancelling the work before the
final power-off also closes the window where a queued worker could
decrement the count to zero and power the same rails down twice.

Probe creates the retry workqueue before anything can take a power
reference, since dropping one queues onto it, and its error paths drop
the probe-time reference instead of leaving the hardware powered across a
reprobe.

The unwinds check what they undo. A domain that will not go down still
has registers behind the clocks and rails below it, so the unwind stops
there and leaks rather than pulling them; and a failed rate restore only
tears down once pm_runtime_put_sync() confirms the device really
suspended.

Ownership across a failed unwind. Three paths deliberately leave resources
enabled - a domain that will not go down, on either the power-on or the
power-off side, and a device that will not suspend - and rknpu_power_get()
was releasing its reference anyway, so the count described powered
hardware as off and the next get would enable it a second time.

Holding the count instead is only half an answer: nothing owns that
reference either, and a retry cannot resume from where those paths stop -
it would put a runtime PM reference power-on had already released, or a
domain that is already down. So they latch power_wedged, which says the
NPU is powered with a transition half-done. Acquisitions are refused from
there rather than handed a device whose state is not known, the retry
worker stands down, and probe and remove take the route that leaves the
domains attached and runtime PM enabled.

Power-off's own domain releases were unchecked, so a domain that stayed up
was followed by the clocks and rails going down underneath it - the case
the power-on unwind already refuses. They are checked against the same
rule now. Its regulators also came down in the order they were enabled;
they follow the power-on unwind and go in reverse.

Remove closes the ioctl paths before quiescing the worker, since they take
power references and one arriving afterwards would queue a retry onto a
workqueue that is already gone. Everything else is freed either way -
removal cannot be declined - but if the final power-off fails the domains
stay attached and runtime PM stays enabled, because yanking the suppliers
out from under live hardware is worse than leaking them.

Probe's failure paths undo pm_runtime_enable() and the named domain
attach, which they were leaving behind, and follow the same rule about
not detaching suppliers when the hardware could not be powered down.

The retry itself is bounded: a floor under the debugfs-settable delay so
zero cannot busy-loop the workqueue, and a cap so a persistent failure
complains once instead of retrying and logging for ever.

